00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.070 align:start position:0%
through the favelas of rio de janeiro a  funeral procession  for two little girls  the cries of mourners heavy not just  with grief but with rage  a devastated community watched on as  their coffins were buried  rebecca beatrix rodriguez dos santos was  seven years old  her cousin emily victoria silva dos  santos was just  four both killed by stray bullets as  they played in the street  emily's father sealed her grave with his  own hands  his fury palpable  and that's because this family blamed  the police for these deaths  they say they saw officers firing into  the community and they want justice  a police officer that says he's prepared  and should protect us  and then shoots in the direction of four  small children  i can't get it straight in my mind my  head i really can't understand this  police operations in rio favelas are an  almost daily occurrence  and almost always violent  the criminal drug gangs they seek are  well armed but the people living here  are poor  and too often caught in the crossfire  one charity claims 22 children have been  hit just this year  eight have died  in general it is boys and girls that  live in favelas who die  that's why these deaths are ignored by  the authorities  the police deny involvement this time  but it's unlikely to wash  in these embattled communities they say  officers shoot first and ask  questions second and it's costing the  most innocent of lives  helena smith sky news
 

